<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trans-Siberian Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trans-Siberian Railway is the railway line linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-russia">European Russia</a> with the Pacific coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a>, the longest in the world. Construction began in 1891, and the line was largely in operation by 1904. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> treat it as a major infrastructure project that integrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the Far East into the imperial economy and supported Russian expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway.jpg" alt="Trans-Siberian Railway" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sorovas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VL_85-022_container_train.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>'s development was hampered by poor transport. Apart from the Great Siberian Route, roads suitable for wheeled traffic were rare, and for about five months of the year rivers were the main means of travel, with horse-drawn sledges over frozen winter roads. Steamboats appeared on the Ob in 1844, but the river systems ran mostly north to south, making west to east transport difficult.</p><p>The first Siberian railway projects emerged after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>–Moscow Railway opened in 1851, among them a proposal by the American entrepreneur Perry Collins. The central government largely ignored them before 1880, and the design of the line then took ten years. On 9 March 1891 the government issued an imperial rescript announcing a railway across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and Tsarevich Nicholas, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>, inaugurated construction at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a> on 19 May that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The line was divided into seven sections worked on at once by 62,000 workers, with an estimated cost of £35 million. To save money and avoid disputes with landowners it was laid outside existing cities: the railway crossed the Ob at Novonikolaevsk, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosibirsk">Novosibirsk</a>, 70 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomsk">Tomsk</a>, which got only a dead-end branch. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a> was crossed by the ice-breaking train ferry SS Baikal, built in 1897 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a>, until the Circum-Baikal Railway was completed in 1904. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amur-river">Amur River</a> line, finished in 1916, made the railway continuous from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904–1905 exposed the limits of a single-track line, where trains waited in sidings to pass one another; this limited capacity and contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s defeat. After 1917 the railway was the main line of communication for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-legion">Czechoslovak Legion</a> and the Allied forces in the Siberian Intervention. The whole length was double-tracked by 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Siberian grain began moving west on the railway, and from 1896 to 1913 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> exported on average about 501,932 metric tons of grain and flour a year. Between 1906 and 1914 about four million peasants migrated to Siberia from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. The historian Christian Wolmar judges the railway a failure, built for narrow political reasons and to a deficient standard, with flimsy rails and rotting ties; others stress the commercial and political importance of its Manchurian section, the Chinese Eastern Railway.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the railway carried Soviet industry eastward from 1941 and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, served as a route for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lend-lease">Lend-Lease</a> supplies. Electrification began in 1929 and was completed in 2002. The main line runs 9,289 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a> across eight time zones, with branches known as the Trans-Manchurian and Trans-Mongolian routes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trans-Siberian Railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…by Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, Edvard Beneš and the Slovak astronomer and general Milan Rastislav Štefánik, whose Czechoslovak National Council in Paris won Allied recognition in 1918, helped by the fame of the Czechoslovak Legion, which had fought its way along the <b>Trans-Siberian Railway</b> during the Russian Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Trans-Siberian Railway</b>, begun in 1891 and substantially completed by 1904, was the most ambitious infrastructure project in the empire's history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
